WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:06.440
Featuring multiple cancellations, a surprising
reveal, and lots of propane and propane accessories,

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:12.620
Hank, Peggy, and Bobby's journey to the "King
of the Hill" finale has been long and eventful.

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:18.250
"King of the Hill" holds up due in no small
part to its forgiving attitude toward the

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:20.500
peculiarities of its characters.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:25.680
The show makes no bones about their shortcomings,
but it always gives them space to grow.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:30.920
Many of the characters are based on personalities
from series creator Mike Judge's childhood,

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:36.250
giving the whole show a sense of down-to-earth
realism that was rare in comedies of its time.

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:40.540
Jokes that would have quickly descended into
cheap shots in most other sitcoms have a way

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:44.460
of disarming themselves instead over the course
of episodes.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:50.250
The series almost always leads with a message
of acceptance, though its also never afraid

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:53.370
to poke fun at the over-the-top traits of
its ensemble.

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:56.690
"Why do you have to hate what you don't understand?"

00:00:56.690 --> 00:00:58.880
"I don't hate you, Bobby!"

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:03.250
Hank Hill's family and friends feel like people
you might meet at the grocery store or at

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:04.549
a company picnic.

00:01:04.549 --> 00:01:09.050
The relationship between Peggy and Hank is
impressively committed and supportive, and

00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:15.049
any apprehension they feel about Bobby's outlandish
interests and decisions is eventually outweighed

00:01:15.049 --> 00:01:18.299
by their desire to let him express himself.

00:01:18.299 --> 00:01:22.970
Despite occasional slow episodes or jokes
that don't land, the cleverness of "King of

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:27.930
the Hill" often lies in its refusal to pretend
to be anything but what it is, while also

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:32.399
being more complex than audiences might guess
at first.

00:01:32.399 --> 00:01:36.750
After such a lengthy run, it's perhaps more
surprising that "King of the Hill" lasted

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:40.730
as long as it did and was only canceled in
Season 13.

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:46.750
According to Mike Judge, the series was essentially
canceled in 2005, only for Fox to surprise

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:49.290
the crew by ordering more episodes.

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:55.000
The episode originally intended to close out
the series was Season 11's "Lucky's Wedding

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:00.320
Suit," which sees Lucky and Luanne get married
alongside a subplot involving a frivolous

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:01.320
lawsuit.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:06.719
However, the show was renewed yet again, making
the episode nothing more than the Season 11

00:02:06.719 --> 00:02:07.719
finale.

00:02:07.719 --> 00:02:12.550
By the time "King of the Hill" was actually
canceled, the show had already faced death

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:14.140
several times.

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:18.769
Though the abrupt end may have disappointed
viewers, Judge seemed fine with it, noting

00:02:18.769 --> 00:02:19.770
to The Chicago Tribune —

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:22.019
"I think it's a good time to stop.

00:02:22.019 --> 00:02:25.980
I'd rather stop when it's still decent, rather
than run it to the ground.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:30.800
It'll be a little weird – this will be the
first time since 1992 that I haven't had a

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:31.800
show on the air.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:33.069
But I'm OK with it."

00:02:33.069 --> 00:02:38.260
Even some of the most popular sitcoms in TV
history have been criticized for having less

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:43.780
than satisfying conclusions, but the episode
"To Sirloin with Love" grants fans a fulfilling

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:48.810
final note by showing the sincere sense of
support and community that surrounds the Hills.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:53.560
With Peggy spending much of the episode off
doing her own thing as Hank and Bobby go through

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:58.520
a whole emotional arc, this episode of "King
of the Hill" evades the standard feelings

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:02.470
of emptiness and lack of direction that many
finales suffer from.

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:06.110
"Usually your pep talks go in one ear and
out the other… but this time, I'm feeling

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:08.860
the pep!"

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:14.480
The charm of the "To Sirloin with Love" conclusion
is that it feels just like any other episode,

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:18.330
but it just so happens to knock it out of
the park by hitting on many of the show's

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:19.750
recurring themes.

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:24.159
Though the early parts of the episode feature
some of the best Hank and Bobby dialogue in

00:03:24.159 --> 00:03:28.709
the whole series, the concluding moments take
it a bit easier by allowing the animation

00:03:28.709 --> 00:03:30.189
to do the talking.

00:03:30.189 --> 00:03:34.450
Scenes of the whole neighborhood coming out
of their homes one by one to enjoy a well-cooked

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:40.290
meal together tie together a show that, even
in its roughest moments, is about the transformative

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:43.080
power of loving your neighbors for who they
are.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:48.230
The dynamic between a conservative father
and his free-spirited son makes up much of

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:52.840
the central focus of "King of the Hill," so
it might not be a huge surprise that the finale

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:55.690
takes their relationship as its central focus.

00:03:55.690 --> 00:04:00.640
Over the course of the series, Hank and Bobby
have their bond challenged in many ways — but

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:05.460
even without outside instability pushing things
to a head, there's the simple problem that

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:07.870
the two just don't have much in common.

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:12.930
While the show often sees them making peace
with their differences, "To Sirloin with Love"

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:17.160
provides a heartwarming twist by finally giving
them a shared interest.

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:21.810
When Peggy announces that she'll be off with
friends for the night, Hank is concerned,

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:25.940
noting that he and Bobby don't have all that
much they can talk about without her around.

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:30.570
However, they connect at a restaurant when
Bobby correctly identifies a flaw in the steak

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:35.130
on his plate, showing Hank that he is a top-notch
purveyor of meats.

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:38.900
"Everybody knows the tiny bone side of the
sirloin is the most tender.

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:39.900
What kind of steak joint is this?!"

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:47.289
A man at a nearby table agrees, introducing
himself as the coach of the Heimlich County

00:04:47.289 --> 00:04:52.020
Junior College meat examination team, which
he asks Bobby to join.

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:57.639
When Bobby asks for his dad's approval, Hank
is so overwhelmed with joy that he immediately

00:04:57.639 --> 00:04:58.759
agrees.

00:04:58.759 --> 00:05:03.080
Just like that, Bobby's on the team, and Hank
has never been happier.

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:07.970
Keeping in step with the core format of "King
of the Hill," Bobby soon experiences doubts

00:05:07.970 --> 00:05:12.080
about the team his father is so overjoyed
to see him become a part of.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:17.300
Though Bobby immediately proves himself a
skilled grader by noticing a detail in a cow

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:22.169
carcass that his teammates don't immediately
see, he clocks them as being a little weird

00:05:22.169 --> 00:05:23.520
right off the bat.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:27.590
When they go out the night before they're
meant to compete, they tell Bobby he can't

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:30.770
jinx them by eating meat before the event.

00:05:30.770 --> 00:05:35.780
After that, they throw red pepper flakes in
the faces of their competitors, shocking Bobby.

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:40.732
However, his coach encourages this behavior,
making Bobby question his future with the

00:05:40.732 --> 00:05:41.732
group.

00:05:41.732 --> 00:05:45.530
"Did you know that no one can hear you scream
from inside a meat locker?”

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:50.560
When Bobby makes a simple mistake and the
others deride and ostracize him, he tells

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:52.590
Hank he has to quit the team.

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:58.220
This causes Hank to have somewhat of an overreaction,
as he was so excited to finally bond with

00:05:58.220 --> 00:05:59.220
his son.

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:03.530
This isn't so much about the competition as
it is about their relationship — a point

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:08.130
that's driven home when Bobby sees the grill
his dad has bought for him in celebration.

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:12.770
Peggy tells him in no uncertain terms that
Hank's disappointment comes from the happiness

00:06:12.770 --> 00:06:18.210
he felt when he realized that he could finally
share an important part of his life with Bobby.

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:22.840
Even though Hank's feelings of sadness around
Bobby's desire to quit the meat examination

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:27.490
team are justified, Bobby has every right
to leave the strange group.

00:06:27.490 --> 00:06:32.949
The shady tactics they resort to in order
to claim a win are only the tip of the iceberg.

00:06:32.949 --> 00:06:37.380
Interactions with the others show Bobby that
there's something deeply weird about every

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:39.259
one of the people on the team.

00:06:39.259 --> 00:06:44.720
The focus on competition over enjoyment unsettles
him, and plays a major role in his decision

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:45.980
to step away.

00:06:45.980 --> 00:06:50.300
It's clear that Bobby not only has a good
sense of ethics, but also a healthy ability

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:52.020
to keep his ego in check.

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:57.199
Hank's pride in Bobby is adorable, and his
stubbornness eventually wanes as he realizes

00:06:57.199 --> 00:07:00.199
just how right Bobby was about the team.

00:07:00.199 --> 00:07:04.970
Finally understanding his son's desire to
quit, Hank learns a classic "King of the Hill"

00:07:04.970 --> 00:07:09.479
lesson — but "To Sirloin with Love" makes
it easier on him than usual.

00:07:09.479 --> 00:07:14.470
He quickly realizes the error of his ways
and seeks to rectify his mistakes.

00:07:14.470 --> 00:07:18.870
Although "To Sirloin with Love" is technically
not the final episode of "King of the Hill"

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:23.620
— more on that later — it's hard to imagine
a more fitting end to the series.

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:28.139
The story ends with Hank and Bobby sharing
a delightful moment as they feed the neighborhood

00:07:28.139 --> 00:07:29.289
together.

00:07:29.289 --> 00:07:33.759
Even though the two don't always see eye to
eye, their shared love of grilling can always

00:07:33.759 --> 00:07:35.009
bring them back together.

00:07:35.009 --> 00:07:37.660
"Oh, you're just getting started, Bobby.

00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:39.730
You'll be grilling your whole life."

00:07:39.730 --> 00:07:42.750
"Just like you."

00:07:42.750 --> 00:07:43.760
"Yeeup."

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:44.770
"Yeeup."

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:48.169
Despite Hank's consistent, series-defining
declaration —

00:07:48.169 --> 00:07:50.909
"That boy ain't right."

00:07:50.909 --> 00:07:56.000
Bobby is arguably the most "right" person
in "King of the Hill," and he proves it in

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:57.039
the finale.

00:07:57.039 --> 00:08:01.400
Not only does he show surprising maturity
in pleading his case with the waitstaff when

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:05.680
he realizes his steak isn't what it should
be, but he's also a great team member who's

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:09.470
capable of working with the goals of a larger
group in mind.

00:08:09.470 --> 00:08:14.380
Bobby's calm and friendly demeanor doesn't
seem as special as it is until we see it contrasted

00:08:14.380 --> 00:08:18.140
with people who have such a long way to go
in their own development.

00:08:18.140 --> 00:08:23.289
If Bobby's arc had to come to a close, this
was the time and place for it to happen.

00:08:23.289 --> 00:08:29.020
Though he was always capable of being profound
beyond his years, here he's able to forgive

00:08:29.020 --> 00:08:33.380
and connect with his dad even when Hank is
being a bit immature.

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:38.269
Besides that, he puts aside his own feelings
of resentment toward the team that treated

00:08:38.269 --> 00:08:43.140
him badly, stepping in where he's needed so
that they're able to succeed in the competition

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:46.510
— even though he no longer wants anything
to do with it.

00:08:46.510 --> 00:08:51.820
Juggling his father's expectations, the need
for personal boundaries, and his own self-interest

00:08:51.820 --> 00:08:56.410
successfully is the kind of thing that has
us taking notes on how to be more like Bobby

00:08:56.410 --> 00:08:57.660
Hill.

00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:02.220
A consistent truth in "King of the Hill" is
that you should keep things honest no matter

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:04.230
how much you want to win.

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:08.140
That message comes back in a big way in "To
Sirloin with Love."

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:13.730
Bobby notices the shady dynamics of the meat
examination team early on, and he finds himself

00:09:13.730 --> 00:09:18.750
completely unwilling to engage in their over-the-top
attempts to hurt their competitors.

00:09:18.750 --> 00:09:23.500
Though we know by now that Bobby is a good
kid who will follow his own ethics above any

00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:29.120
group dynamics, his intuition has been honed
into something truly impressive by the series'

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:30.120
conclusion.

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:34.910
This episode is far from the only competition-gone-wrong
to appear on "King of the Hill," but it is

00:09:34.910 --> 00:09:37.470
one of the most revealing for its characters.

00:09:37.470 --> 00:09:42.010
The added stress on Bobby to do well with
the team so his father will continue to be

00:09:42.010 --> 00:09:46.510
proud of him is extreme, but even despite
Hank's disappointment, there's no question

00:09:46.510 --> 00:09:48.500
that he truly loves his son.

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:53.580
Earlier in the episode, when Bobby gets upset
with himself for only getting fourth place,

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:58.720
Hank is overjoyed, celebrating his fourth-place
slot with a level of enthusiasm that can't

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:00.260
help but be endearing.

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:04.860
The knowledge that Hank doesn't care whether
Bobby succeeds — only that they have a common

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:09.640
interest — only pushes forward the enduring
message of fair play that pops up so often

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:11.269
throughout the series.

00:10:11.269 --> 00:10:17.089
Among Hank Hill's beer-drinking buddies, Boomhauer
is always the most mysterious one, with his

00:10:17.089 --> 00:10:22.279
slurred, rapid-fire way of talking making
it nearly impossible to understand him.

00:10:22.279 --> 00:10:26.780
"It dang ol' complicated, you know, man, it's
like a dang ol' Rubik's cube man, like, talking

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:30.770
about blue, red, man, then you get to one
side and you, like, messed up the other side,

00:10:30.770 --> 00:10:31.770
man."

00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:36.200
Initially being pretty one-dimensional, Boomhauer
is often defined by his interest in women

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:40.649
and muscle cars, though his luck with both
is regularly called into question when the

00:10:40.649 --> 00:10:43.560
series starts unpacking the details of his
life.

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:48.310
Regardless, Boomhauer is highly astute and
capable of giving calm and reasonable advice

00:10:48.310 --> 00:10:51.589
to others, making him a helpful guy to have
around.

00:10:51.589 --> 00:10:56.769
By the end of the series, Boomhauer gets a
few spotlight episodes and even some fairly

00:10:56.769 --> 00:11:00.160
profound moments with characters like Bobby
and Hank.

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:05.310
After spending the whole series without ever
actually stating what his job is, the last

00:11:05.310 --> 00:11:09.930
episodes tell us that Hank's bestie Boomhauer
was really a Texas Ranger the whole time.

00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:14.959
This revelation occurs in a casual scene in
which Boomhauer smells the grill outdoors

00:11:14.959 --> 00:11:17.079
and leaves his home to join in.

00:11:17.079 --> 00:11:21.540
His wallet pops open, revealing a badge and
solving one of the show's big mysteries in

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:23.700
a perfectly low-key way.

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:28.620
Does this mean that Boomhauer was casually
spying on anti-government conspiracy theorist

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:29.960
Dale throughout their friendship?

00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:31.660
We may never know.

00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:36.820
"To Sirloin with Love" acts as the finale
to "King of the Hill," but it wasn't the last

00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:38.519
episode to actually air.

00:11:38.519 --> 00:11:43.850
Four more episodes that had already been produced
premiered in syndication after the fact: "The

00:11:43.850 --> 00:11:48.230
Honeymooners," "Bill Gathers Moss," "When
Joseph Met Lori, and Made Out with Her in

00:11:48.230 --> 00:11:52.410
the Janitor's Closet," and "Just Another Manic
Kahn-Day."

00:11:52.410 --> 00:11:57.110
These are more like ordinary episodes than
series finales, but that doesn't mean they

00:11:57.110 --> 00:11:59.000
aren't still fun to watch.

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:04.360
Hank's mother announces her intention to marry
a man Hank hasn't even met, Bill decides to

00:12:04.360 --> 00:12:09.550
combat his depression by getting some roommates,
and Joseph's love life picks up steam.

00:12:09.550 --> 00:12:14.250
In "Just Another Manic Kahn-Day," the last
episode to air chronologically, the often

00:12:14.250 --> 00:12:19.320
combative relationship between Hank and his
neighbor Kahn gains a new dimension as the

00:12:19.320 --> 00:12:22.430
two spend some one-on-one time with each other.

00:12:22.430 --> 00:12:27.410
With Minh and Connie on a trip, Hank is tasked
with checking in on his neighbor.

00:12:27.410 --> 00:12:32.230
Deciding that medication is unnecessary, Hank
tells Kahn to not bother waiting in line at

00:12:32.230 --> 00:12:33.230
the pharmacy.

00:12:33.230 --> 00:12:34.230
"Yeah!

00:12:34.230 --> 00:12:35.230
You're right!

00:12:35.230 --> 00:12:40.370
I think I'll self-medicate with 50,000 BTUs
of ass-kicking hellfire!"

00:12:40.370 --> 00:12:45.899
This sends Kahn into a manic episode in which
he becomes uncharacteristically upbeat and

00:12:45.899 --> 00:12:49.550
wants to work endlessly on a shared grill
project with Hank.

00:12:49.550 --> 00:12:54.670
However, Hank soon realizes he misunderstood
the extent of Kahn's bipolar disorder when

00:12:54.670 --> 00:12:58.579
his neighbor hits a low point and is unable
to complete the project.

00:12:58.579 --> 00:13:03.600
Though Hank doesn't always behave in the best
way, he and Kahn ultimately end up becoming

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:06.550
closer due to their newfound understanding.

00:13:06.550 --> 00:13:09.150
To all good things, there must be an end.

00:13:09.150 --> 00:13:13.980
Even though "King of the Hill" has an excellent
conclusion, it can still feel bittersweet

00:13:13.980 --> 00:13:16.160
to fans who wanted another 13 seasons.

00:13:16.160 --> 00:13:21.260
If that's you, you may be in luck, as there
have been recent rumors of a revival series

00:13:21.260 --> 00:13:25.470
coming from original co-creators Mike Judge
and Greg Daniels.

00:13:25.470 --> 00:13:30.060
Though very little information has been released
to date around what we could expect from a

00:13:30.060 --> 00:13:35.750
return to Arlen, chances are it would involve
quite a lot of talk of propane and propane

00:13:35.750 --> 00:13:38.949
accessories — and we wouldn't want it any
other way.

00:13:38.949 --> 00:13:44.720
In January of 2022, Daniels told Collider
that his and Judge's idea for a revival would

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:48.800
start with a time jump, aging up the show's
characters significantly.

00:13:48.800 --> 00:13:53.800
However, it's still unclear whether or not
the revival will actually happen.

00:13:53.800 --> 00:13:58.480
In September of 2022, it was announced that
Fox was uninterested in bringing the series

00:13:58.480 --> 00:14:02.690
back, leaving Judge and Daniels without a
definite home for their reboot.

00:14:02.690 --> 00:14:07.990
"King of the Hill" could still be coming back,
however, and Judge and Daniels seem excited

00:14:07.990 --> 00:14:09.750
to bring it to life.

00:14:09.750 --> 00:14:14.470
Spending years with the Hills was a formative
experience for a lot of sitcom fans, so here's

00:14:14.470 --> 00:14:19.300
hoping the new series will be every bit the
low-key slice-of-life we've come to know and

00:14:19.300 --> 00:14:19.380
love.

